    <item>
      <title>Kelvin 0.3.0 — point at the thing you want to edit</title>
      <pubDate>Tue, 28 Jul 2026 22:04:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <sparkle:version>247</sparkle:version>
      <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.3.0</sparkle:shortVersionString>
      <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
      <link>https://github.com/wmwallace/Kelvin/releases/tag/v0.3.0</link>
      <!-- The first release whose notes reach the updater. Sparkle renders this HTML in its own
           panel, so it is self-contained: no external fonts, images or scripts, and it reads in
           both light and dark. Every earlier item shipped without a description, which meant an
           in-app update offered 1.4 GB and said nothing about what was in it. -->
      <description><![CDATA[
        <style>
          /* Kelvin update notes. Self-contained: no external fonts, images or scripts — Sparkle
             renders this offline, mid-install, in a narrow pane. */
          :root {
            color-scheme: light dark;
            --ink: #1c1a17;
            --dim: #6b6660;
            --faint: #928c84;
            --bg: #faf8f5;
            --rule: rgba(0,0,0,0.10);
            --glow: #b4661a;
          }
          @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
            :root {
              --ink: #e8e3dc;
              --dim: #9a938a;
              --faint: #6f6862;
              --bg: #131211;
              --rule: rgba(255,255,255,0.11);
              --glow: #e9a44c;
            }
          }
          body {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 18px 20px 26px;
            background: var(--bg);
            color: var(--ink);
            font: 13px/1.55 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "SF Pro Text", system-ui, sans-serif;
            -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
          }
          /* The blackbody rail: amber through daylight to blue, which is what the app is named after
             and the one piece of decoration here that means something. It separates sections the way
             the temperature rail runs under the canvas in the app itself. */
          .rail {
            height: 2px;
            border: 0;
            margin: 20px 0 16px;
            background: linear-gradient(90deg, #e9a44c 0%, #f2ead9 42%, #cfd8e6 68%, #7fa2cc 100%);
            opacity: 0.85;
          }
          .lede {
            margin: 0;
            font-size: 15px;
            line-height: 1.45;
            letter-spacing: -0.005em;
          }
          .version {
            font: 10px/1 ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, monospace;
            letter-spacing: 0.14em;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            color: var(--faint);
            margin: 0 0 9px;
          }
          /* Mono, tracked, uppercase — the same label treatment the app uses over its own panel
             sections, so the notes read in the app's voice rather than a changelog's. */
          h2 {
            font: 10px/1 ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, monospace;
            letter-spacing: 0.16em;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            color: var(--glow);
            margin: 22px 0 7px;
          }
          h2:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
          p { margin: 0 0 9px; }
          p.note { color: var(--dim); }
          ul { margin: 0 0 9px; padding-left: 17px; }
          li { margin-bottom: 4px; }
          strong { font-weight: 600; }
          .limit {
            border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
            padding-left: 11px;
            margin: 12px 0 0;
            color: var(--dim);
          }
          .closing {
            margin-top: 22px;
            padding-top: 14px;
            border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
            color: var(--faint);
            font-size: 12px;
          }
        </style>

        <p class="version">Kelvin 0.3.0</p>
        <p class="lede">Point at the thing you want to edit — and take it back off again.</p>

        <hr class="rail">

        <h2>Select anything, not just what Kelvin recognises</h2>
        <p>Subject detection finds whatever stands out most and stops. On a coastal frame that means it
        hands back the big sea stack and neither of the two smaller ones beside it. Those had nothing to
        click.</p>
        <p><strong>The wand</strong> fills that gap. Click the thing you want and the selection spreads
        from there until the picture stops matching, staying inside whatever you pointed at. It is stored
        as the point you clicked plus a tolerance — never as pixels — so the region is regrown at whatever
        size is being rendered. The selection you approve on screen is the one that reaches a full-resolution
        export.</p>
        <p class="limit">It will not do everything. Where the thing you clicked touches something else the
        same colour, the fill walks straight into it. No tolerance fixes that, because the path between them
        really is the same colour. Kelvin says so when it happens, and the brush below is how you fix it.</p>

        <h2>The brush can subtract</h2>
        <p>Every mask was built by adding, so one that grabbed too much had to be deleted and drawn again.
        The brush now has <strong>Add</strong> and <strong>Erase</strong>. Paint over the rock, switch to
        Erase, take the spill back off the sky.</p>
        <p>A soft brush erases softly — the edge is scaled rather than cut, so taking part of a feathered
        mask away does not leave a hard-edged hole in it.</p>

        <h2>Best actually filters now</h2>
        <p>Best shows one frame per group of alike ones, the sharpest of each. It was reading measurements
        that nothing ever started, so on a shoot you had not separately scanned it returned everything, and
        waiting did not help. Choosing it now asks for the measurement it needs — as do Focus and Flagged —
        and opening a different shoot measures that one too.</p>
        <p class="note">On a 437-frame shoot that is 148 groups: two thirds of it collapses.</p>

        <h2>Export says what it will do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The size button no longer lies.</strong> It read “Export full-res” while honouring a
          size you chose once and forgot. It now names the size it will write.</li>
          <li><strong>The group button names a scope</strong>, not a bare number, and counts what will
          really be written.</li>
          <li><strong>A selection is the scope.</strong> Export was the one action that ignored selected
          frames. Select four and it exports four.</li>
          <li><strong>Filename prefix and suffix are remembered</strong>, alongside the naming scheme — and
          the panel no longer disagrees with itself about the name it is about to use.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="closing">Everything runs on your Mac. No account, no upload, and nothing leaves the
        machine except this update check — which you can switch off in Settings.</p>
      ]]></description>
